export const modernPerspective = [
  'Localized-basis convergence is multidimensional. Radial multiplicity, polarization channels, diffuse functions, confinement radii, angular cutoffs, integration grids, auxiliary bases, and the treatment of core states can affect different observables at different rates. A stable total energy therefore does not guarantee converged forces, eigenvalue splittings, response functions, or energy differences between unlike environments.',
  'Finite atom-centered bases can also produce basis-set superposition error in binding and adsorption energies: two fragments gain variational flexibility when they share each other’s functions. Systematic basis families, counterpoise-style diagnostics where appropriate, and direct basis convergence are ways to expose this effect; the issue is distinct from exchange–correlation or finite-size error.',
  'Pulay terms are a structural consequence of a geometry-dependent finite basis. They vanish in the ideal limit in which the variational space is complete or independent of nuclear coordinates, not merely because an SCF cycle is tightly converged. Force and stress validation must therefore test both electronic convergence and basis-response terms.',
  'Modern localized-orbital implementations often combine the ideas in this chapter with sparse matrices, density fitting or resolution-of-identity approximations, hierarchical numerical atom-centered bases, selected Green-function techniques, and linear-scaling algorithms. These accelerations change the numerical route without changing the need to state the represented subspace and converge the target observable.',
];

export const synthesis =
  'Chapter 15 turns the localized matrix language of tight binding into a self-consistent first-principles method. The Hamiltonian and overlap matrices are now generated from explicit basis functions and the Kohn–Sham potential rather than supplied as fitted parameters. That change makes basis design part of the numerical approximation: Gaussian functions trade atomic realism for analytic integral technology, numerical orbitals trade universal formulas for direct control over radial shape and spatial range, and both require explicit checks of completeness, nonorthogonality, integration, and basis motion. The density matrix provides a compact bridge from orbital coefficients to density, total energy, forces, and stress, while Pulay terms reveal the special responsibility created when basis functions move with the nuclei. Green-function and mixed-basis constructions show how locality can be exploited or combined with delocalized representations. Chapter 16 now approaches the same electronic-structure problem from a different direction, augmenting simple interstitial functions with atom-centered partial waves and scattering information near the nuclei.';
